WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:10.646
"Does Sunscreen Cause or Prevent Skin Cancer?"

00:00:10.777 --> 00:00:13.080
Decades ago,
studies started bubbling up,

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.060
reporting that not only
was the use of sunscreen

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.980
not found to be protective
against skin cancer,

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.160
but it was associated with
significantly elevated risk.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.000
But subsequent studies offered
the obvious explanation

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:24.900
of this paradox.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.920
People who use sunscreen are
more likely to be sunbathers.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.860
That's like finding those
who use motorcycle helmets

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.360
are more likely to die
on the highway.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.240
It's not that sunscreen
and helmets aren't protective;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:37.440
it's just
that they're associated

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.480
with engagement
in risky activities.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:44.100
But you don't know,
until you put it to the test.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.160
As I noted in my last video,
randomized controlled trials

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.020
have shown that regular use
of sunscreen

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.420
can arrest visible signs
of skin aging,

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.520
including
biopsy-proven reductions

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.080
in UV-related skin damage.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:56.880
But are there
interventional trials

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.100
proving sunscreen
can prevent cancer?

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.360
Yes.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.760
Organ transplant recipients

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.680
are highly susceptible
to skin cancers

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.020
because they have to be given
immune suppressants

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:08.520
to prevent organ rejection.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.160
A group of 120 organ recipients

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.680
were equally informed
about sun protection measures,

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.420
but half were given free

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.880
broad spectrum
SPF 50+ sunscreen

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.860
for daily application
to their head, neck,

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:20.940
forearms, and hands.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:24.660
After two years, there were
nine new basal carcinomas

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:26.220
in the control group
versus only two

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.120
in the sunscreen group.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.880
That sounds good, but it may
have just been a fluke.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.820
In contrast, there was
a highly significant difference

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.080
in the proportion of patients

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.480
who got new invasive
squamous cell carcinomas.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.160
Eight new cases
in the control group

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.140
versus zero
in the sunscreen group.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:44.400
But wait until you hear
about what happened

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.020
to their actinic keratoses,

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:49.800
the precancerous growths
that can turn into skin cancer.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.620
They all started out
covered in them,

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:55.200
a total of 191 detected
in each group.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.120
In the subsequent 24 months,

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.580
the control group
developed 82 new ones.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:02.160
How many did
the sunscreen group develop?

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.620
Negative 102.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.860
More than 100
precancerous growths

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.860
in the sunscreen group
spontaneously regressed

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:14.820
and vanished completely
(compared to 82 new growths

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:15.900
developing in the control group

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.400
without a single one
disappearing).

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.820
Their bodies, even in
their immunocompromised state,

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:23.940
could heal itself
once it just stopped

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.460
being bombarded with
so many cancer-causing rays.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:28.920
Other randomized
controlled trials

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.180
have shown similar findings,

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.240
but generalizability
is limited.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.640
The studies all used
high-risk populations,

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.980
for example, those living
in subtropical climates,

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.680
or with a personal history
of precancerous lesions.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.100
So, cancer prevention efficacy
in the general population

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:45.720
would be expected to be less.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.500
On the other hand, the studies
could also have underestimated

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.960
the impact since they were
relatively short-term

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.160
(not exceeding four years)

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.880
and, ethically, had to allow
the control group participants

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:58.740
to slather on
their own sunscreen,

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:01.500
which would dilute
the difference between groups.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.120
For maximum effectiveness,

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.400
sunscreen needs to be
applied properly,

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.400
which apparently rarely happens
in the real world.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.520
In a study of nearly 5,000
skiers and snowboarders,

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:12.480
almost no one,

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.120
only about 4 percent
were fully compliant

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:16.140
with sunscreen recommendations.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.540
First, there's the amount.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:20.820
The FDA standard
is two milligrams

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.260
per square centimeter.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:23.700
What does that mean?

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.040
Use the teaspoon rule:

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.440
one teaspoon for your face,
head and neck,

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.600
one for the front
of your torso,

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.940
another for your back,
one teaspoon for each arm

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:34.680
and two teaspoons for each leg.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.600
That's a total
of nine teaspoons.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.220
That's about
the total volume equivalent

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:42.240
of a golf ball or shot glass,
to help you visualize it.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.360
Unfortunately,
the average sunscreen user

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.640
may only apply a quarter
of the recommended amount.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.880
This is why high SPF sunscreens
are often recommended,

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.620
like 50+ SPF.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:56.220
Although the FDA recommends
a minimum SPF of only 15

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.540
to prevent skin cancer,

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:00.240
under normal
consumer conditions,

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.180
even an SPF of 50
may effectively give you

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:04.560
only an SPF of 12.5,

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.720
because most people
don't put on enough.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.000
Randomized double-blind,

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.980
head-to-head,
split-face experiments,

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.500
where you're randomized
to apply one sunscreen

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:14.280
to one side of your face

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:15.960
and another sunscreen
to the other side,

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.000
show that in real life use,

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.960
SPF 100+ sunscreen works
significantly better

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:24.300
than SPF 50+ sunscreen.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.520
More than 50 percent
of the participants

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.160
were sunburned more
on their SPF 50+ side

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.440
compared to only
about 5 percent

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.480
on their SPF 100+ side.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.960
Now, a potential downside
of higher SPF sunscreens

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.480
is that they could provide
a false sense of security.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:42.420
Those randomized
to an SPF 30 sunscreen

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:44.880
ended up spending
more cumulative time in the sun

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:47.340
than those receiving
an SPF 10 sunscreen,

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.440
as much as five times longer
in some cases.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:51.240
What else?

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:53.820
Well, people tend to remember
to use sunscreen

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:54.960
on a sunny day at the beach,

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.660
but sun protection is needed
even on cloudy days,

00:04:57.660 --> 00:04:59.520
since the UV rays
are not dampened

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:00.720
as much as visible light.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:04.080
There's even a phenomenon
known as "cloud enhancement"

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:06.240
where overcast skies
can sometimes result

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.760
in even more UV reaching
the Earth's surface

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:10.080
compared to clear skies.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.200
Sunscreen labels often suggest
waiting at least 15 minutes

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.120
after application
before going outdoors,

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:16.920
but when put to the test,

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:19.140
sunscreen was found
to start working immediately

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:22.080
with the full effect apparent
by minute ten.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.540
However, if water resistance
is required,

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:25.740
it may be prudent to wait

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:28.080
the full 15 to 30 minutes
after application

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:29.460
before taking a dip.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.580
The terms "waterproof"
or "sweatproof"

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:34.440
appear to be
meaningless marketing,

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.180
as no difference
in retention was noted

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:39.120
between "waterproof" products
and those that were

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:40.860
merely labeled
"water-resistant."

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:42.360
Both were better than

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:44.040
non-water-resistant products,
though,

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:46.380
which lost nearly
all their protective effects

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:48.660
within 20 minutes
of water immersion.

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:51.960
The suggestion that sunscreen
should be reapplied

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:54.300
every two to three hours
only applies under conditions

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.700
in which it is rinsed off
by water or sweat,

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:59.520
or rubbed off by friction
from clothing or sand.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.300
Even after allowing sunscreen
to dry first for 20 minutes,

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.060
between 15 and 60 percent
of its protective effect

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:08.580
can be lost
after contact with sand.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:11.520
Otherwise, if the recommended
amount is applied

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:13.260
and the sunscreen layer
is not disturbed,

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:16.620
SPF can be maintained
for as long as eight hours.

